public class ISBNValidator {
	public static boolean isValidISBN(String ISBN) {
		if(ISBN.length() !=13) {
			return false;
		}
		int total = 0;
		for(int i = 0; i <=11; i++) {
			total +=((i+1) * Character.getNumericValue(ISBN.charAt(i)));
		}
		int lastDigit = Character.getNumericValue(ISBN.charAt(ISBN.length() - 1));
		return (total % 11 == lastDigit);
	}
}

The Ackermann Function is a recursive function which can be optimized by using memoization. Memoization provides a way to store previously calculated values and look them up quickly when needed, saving time by avoiding recalculation.

To memoize the Ackermann Function, we can create a dictionary to store the previously calculated values keyed on the input parameters (m, n). If the parameters already exist in the dictionary, we can simply retrieve the result instead of running the function again:

def ackermann(m, n):
 if m == 0:
 return n + 1
 elif n == 0:
 return ackermann(m - 1, 1)
 else:
 # check if parameters already exist in dictionary
 # if so, return the result.
 if (m, n) in ackermann_cache:
 return ackermann_cache[(m, n)]

 # otherwise, calculate the result
 result = ackermann(m - 1, ackermann(m, n - 1))

 # put the result in the cache
 ackermann_cache[(m, n)] = result

 # return the result
 return result

